<p>The Trump administration said U.S. hostilities with Iran have “terminated” for purposes of the War Powers Resolution, a position that could let President Trump avoid seeking congressional authorization after a May 1 legal deadline. The deadline followed joint U.S.-Israeli strikes on Iran that began on Feb. 28 and Trump’s formal notification to Congress on March 2, which started the 60-day clock under the 1973 law. A senior administration official said the United States and Iran agreed to a two-week ceasefire on April 7 that was later extended, and that there had been no exchange of fire between U.S. armed forces and Iran since then. Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth told lawmakers the administration understood the clock to pause or stop during a ceasefire, while Democrats including Sen. Tim Kaine and Senate Democratic leader Chuck Schumer said the statute does not allow such a pause.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/60qyRHsRKbZezQZpvZiICRTeDPEnavSTGVBdXkMkFM2ZeHBqoPRqxXaps3blY3c1Gqi1dqso6I7gK42x0bINCV0GIftuuIuP2XMVSRY7PLNtx1dg4jyBHqK8QOivwq9ZnoLvD-xS-21VUvWEo45nalMDPWoN9mP_TYTPPgoGnRbzXq0kwe6NmpjNI6XhbczLreb7O0CuWyMzDXmsufpl5dllCNZ3IKiVZCDxr4SZ-yTcZvW8NtySJDHxJrYVguCV118w7gY7vny7_GiE1w' alt='Representatives from foreign diplomatic missions, United Nations offices and media view damage at sites previously targeted by U.S.-Israeli strikes in Tehran on April 20.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Congressional efforts: Democrats tried repeatedly after the strikes to force withdrawal or authorization votes, but pro-Trump Republicans with narrow majorities in both chambers voted those measures down.</li><li>Iran posture: Reports said Tehran activated air defenses as the deadline approached.</li><li>Diplomatic backdrop: Iran’s supreme leader signaled unwillingness to give in to Trump’s demands, while nuclear negotiations remained stalled.</li><li>Sanctions pressure: The United States kept economic pressure on Tehran, including newly announced sanctions.</li></ul><